 As a helper assistant, I'd be happy to help you with that! Here's a simple way to find duplicates in a collection using C#. This example uses a `List<T>` as the collection, but you can modify it to fit your specific needs.

```csharp
public void FindDuplicates<T>(List<T> collection)
{
    var duplicates = new HashSet<T>();
    var result = new List<T>();

    foreach (var item in collection)
    {
        if (duplicates.Add(item) == false)
        {
            result.Add(item);
        }
    }

    Console.WriteLine("Duplicates:");
    foreach (var duplicate in result)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(duplicate);
    }
}
```

In this function, we use a `HashSet<T>` to store unique items. When we iterate through the collection, if an item already exists in the `HashSet`, it means it's a duplicate, and we add it to the `result` list.

You can call this function like this:

```csharp
List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 7, 7 };
FindDuplicates(numbers);
```

This will print out the duplicates in the `numbers` list:

