 Here's a simple GO function that calculates the square root of a number using the built-in `math.Sqrt()` function. This function reads a number from the user, checks if the number is non-negative, and returns the square root if the number is valid.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math"
)

func main() {
	var number float64

	fmt.Print("Enter a number: ")
	fmt.Scan(&number)

	if number < 0 {
		fmt.Println("Error: The number must be non-negative.")
	} else {
		fmt.Printf("The square root of %.2f is %.2f\n", number, math.Sqrt(number))
	}
}
```

To run this code, save it in a file named `main.go` and execute it using the `go run` command:

```sh
$ go run main.go
```

Then, enter a number when prompted, and the program will output the square root of the entered number.

 To achieve this, we'll use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's a simple example of how you can underline multiple lines with different colors for a single word.

HTML:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
    <title>Multiple Line Underline</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="underline-container">
        <span class="underlineable word-category-1">Example</span>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

CSS (styles.css):
```css
.underline-container {
    position: relative;
}

.underlineable {
    position: relative;
}

.underline {
    position: absolute;
    width: 100%;
    height: 2px;
}
```

JavaScript (script.js):
```javascript
const underlines = document.querySelectorAll('.underline');

underlines.forEach((underline, index) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
        underline.style.backgroundColor = `hsl(60 + ${index * 30}, 100%, 50%)`;
    }, index * 50);
});
```

In this example, we're using the `setTimeout` function in JavaScript to create a delay between each line's color change. The CSS classes `.underline-container` and `.underlineable` help position the underline elements correctly, while the CSS class `.underline` styles the underline itself. The JavaScript code selects all underlines and changes their colors based on their index.

In your specific case, you can replace "Example" with the word you want to underline and adjust the CSS color values to suit your desired categories. You'll also need to add more `<span>` elements with the `.underline` class to create multiple lines for each word.

Here's an example of how you can add more underlines:

HTML:
```html
<span class="underline word-category-1"></span>
<span class="underline word-category-2"></span>
<span class="underline word-category-1"></span>
```

You'll need to adjust the JavaScript code to ensure each underline gets a unique color based on its index:

JavaScript:
```javascript
const underlines = document.querySelectorAll('.underline');

underlines.forEach((underline, index) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
        underline.style.backgroundColor = `hsl(60 + ${index * 30}, 100%, 50%)`;
    }, index * 50);
});
```

 To create a 3D card view in SwiftUI that uses accelerometer data for a dynamic effect, you can follow these steps:

1. Import the necessary SwiftUI and CoreMotion frameworks:

```swift
import SwiftUI
import CoreMotion

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var xRotation: Double = 0.0
    @State private var yRotation: Double = 0.0
    @State private var zRotation: Double = 0.0

    let motionManager = CMMotionManager()

    var body: some View {
        // Your 3D card view
    }
}
```

2. Set up the motion manager to start updating the accelerometer data:

```swift
    if motionManager.isAccelerometerAvailable {
        motionManager.startAccelerometerUpdates(to: .main) { data, error in
            if let data = data {
                self.xRotation = Double(data.acceleration.x)
                self.yRotation = Double(data.acceleration.y) * -1
                self.zRotation = Double(data.acceleration.z) * -1
            }
        }
    }
```

3. Create the 3D card view using `ZStack` and `Rotation3D` modifiers:

```swift
    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            Rectangle()
                .fill(Color.white)
                .frame(width: 200, height: 300)
                .rotation3DEffect(
                    Angle(degrees: xRotation),
                    axis: (x: 0, y: 1, z: 0)
                )
                .rotation3DEffect(
                    Angle(degrees: yRotation),
                    axis: (x: 1, y: 0, z: 0)
                )
                .rotation3DEffect(
                    Angle(degrees: zRotation),
                    axis: (x: 0, y: 0, z: 1)
                )
        }
    }
```

4. Stop the accelerometer updates when the view is deinitialized:

```swift
    deinit {
        motionManager.stopAccelerometerUpdates()
    }
```

This example creates a simple 3D card view that rotates based on the accelerometer data. You can customize the appearance and behavior of the card view as needed.

 To set up a reverse proxy for a Flask application running on a subdirectory using Nginx, follow these steps:

1. Install Nginx on your server if it's not already installed.

2. Create your Flask application and ensure it's running on the specified subdirectory (e.g., `/app/myapp`).

3. Create a new Nginx configuration file for your application. You can create it in the `/etc/nginx/sites-available/` directory, for example, `myapp.conf`.

4. Edit the new configuration file and add the following content:

```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name your_domain.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost/app/myapp;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `your_domain.com` with the domain name you want to use for this application. Also, ensure that the `/app/myapp` path matches the location of your running Flask application.

5. Create a symbolic link from the configuration file in `/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/` to activate the configuration:

```
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```

6. Test the Nginx configuration:

```
sudo nginx -t
```

If there are no errors, restart Nginx to apply the changes:

```
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```

Now, when you access `your_domain.com` in your browser, it should display your Flask application running on the specified subdirectory.

