 To achieve this in React, you can create a custom React hook that checks if the element's bottom is not visible in the window and scrolls it into view if that condition is met. Here's a simple implementation of such a hook:

```jsx
import React, { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

const useScrollIntoView = (ref) => {
  const element = ref.current;

  useEffect(() => {
    const checkIfVisible = () => {
      const rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();
      const windowHeight = Math.max(
        document.documentElement.clientHeight,
        window.innerHeight || 0
      );
      return rect.bottom > windowHeight;
    };

    if (checkIfVisible()) {
      element.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'end' });
    }
  }, []);

  return ref;
};

// Usage in your component
const MyComponent = () => {
  const elementRef = useRef(null);

  return (
    <div ref={elementRef}>
      // Your element content
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

Now, whenever the component mounts, the hook checks if the element's bottom is not visible in the window. If it is, it will scroll the element into view smoothly. Make sure to wrap your component with a parent element, and pass the ref to the custom hook.

This hook will only trigger the scroll action once, when the component mounts. If you want to update the scroll behavior on component updates, you can modify the dependency array in the `useEffect` hook to include a state variable or a prop.

